{"blocks":[["Music/Scale/","source-block-294","Altered minor",["Music Scale"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-314","Music/Scale/Minor/Harmonic",["Music Scale","Altered minor"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-349","Music/Scale/Minor/Melodic",["Music Scale","Altered minor"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-383","Music/Scale/Minor/Hungarian",["Music Scale","Altered minor"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-419","Music/Scale/Dorian/Ukrainian",["Music Scale","Altered minor"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-456","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note",["Music Scale"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-499","Music/Scale/Pentatonic/Major",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-536","Music/Scale/Pentatonic/Minor",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-573","Music/Scale/Hirajoshi",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-603","Music/Scale/Blues",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-629","Music/Scale/Whole Tone",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-660","Music/Scale/Dorian/Bebop",["Music Scale","Pentatonic, hexatonic and eight-note"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-693","Hindustani thaats",["Music Scale"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-717","Music/Scale/Marwa",["Music Scale","Hindustani thaats"]],["Music/Scale/","source-block-743","Music/Scale/Todi",["Music Scale","Hindustani thaats"]],["Music/Scale/Blues/","source-block-104","Blues Scale",[]],["Music/Scale/Blues/","source-block-120","Also called the minor blues or hexatonic blues scale. On C, in its standard textbook form: C E♭ F G♭ G B♭. Steps in semitones: 3 2 1 1 3 2. Music/Scale/Pentatonic/Minor with an added ♭5.",["Blues Scale"]],["Music/Scale/Blues/","source-block-322","History",["Blues Scale"]],["Music/Scale/Blues/","source-block-336","The blues came out of African American vocal music in the Deep South around 1900. Its \"blue notes\" are inflected regions around the third, fifth and seventh that a singer or guitarist bends into, and they fall between equal-tempered pitches.",["Blues Scale","History"]]],"format_version":5}